Problem Statement: To find the element that appears more than (n/2) times in an array, where (n) is the size of the array
Example: arr = [2, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2]
The majority element is 2 because it appears 5 times, which is more than (7/2 = 3.5).

Brute Force Approach
For every element, count how many times it occurs in the entire array. If its frequency is greater than (n/2), return it.
The outer loop selects each element.
The inner loop counts its occurrences in the entire array.
If the count is greater than \(n/2\), that element is returned.

Code:

int majorityElement(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int count = 0;

        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[i] == arr[j]) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        if (count > n / 2) {
            return arr[i];
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
Time: (O(n^2))
Space: (O(1))


Better Approach :By Using a Hash Map
Store each element's frequency in a map. As soon as an element's frequency becomes greater than \(n/2\), return it.

Code:

int majorityElement(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    unordered_map<int, int> freq;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        freq[arr[i]]++;

        if (freq[arr[i]] > n / 2) {
            return arr[i];
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
Time: (O(n))
Space: (O(n))


Optimal Approach: Moore's Voting Algorithm
Maintain a candidate and a counter. When the counter is zero, select the current element as the candidate. If the next element matches the candidate, increment the counter; otherwise, decrement it.

Code:

int majorityElement(vector<int>& arr) {
    int candidate = 0;
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        if (count == 0) {
            candidate = arr[i];
        }

        if (arr[i] == candidate) {
            count++;
        } else {
            count--;
        }
    }

    return candidate;
}
Time: (O(n)
Space: (O(1))
